WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.267
The serving and cost stack.

2
00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:08.933
This is where token cost and latency optimization lives, a line in every senior job description.

3
00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:11.700
The mental model is simple.

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.500
A request should be answered by the cheapest layer that can answer it.

5
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.033
Cache first, small model second, frontier model last.

6
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:21.733
Here is the routing path.

7
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:23.062
First the cache.

8
00:00:23.062 --> 00:00:27.433
An exact hit is nearly free, and a semantic hit costs one embedding.

9
00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:30.933
On a miss, a small model handles the easy requests.

10
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:36.867
Only hard requests reach the frontier model, which costs ten to a hundred times more per call.

11
00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:39.900
Caching economics are striking.

12
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:45.800
Even a twenty percent hit rate on support traffic pays for the Redis cluster many times over.

13
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.067
Who picks the tier?

14
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:53.633
A cheap classifier or heuristic, using length, keywords, or whether tools are needed.

15
00:00:53.933 --> 00:01:00.433
Easy traffic goes to the small model, hard traffic to frontier, and bulk volume to the self-hosted lane.

16
00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:05.067
Log every decision, and audit misroutes weekly against quality evals.

17
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:07.233
Now the risks.

18
00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:13.533
A near-duplicate query with one changed entity, like March versus April, must not hit the cache.

19
00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:19.367
So use high thresholds, entity checks, and never share personalized answers across users.

20
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:24.433
Self-hosting pays off only past the break-even of volume times tokens.

21
00:01:25.633 --> 00:01:27.333
Finally, fallback design.

22
00:01:27.633 --> 00:01:29.600
Say the main provider starts failing.

23
00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.667
A gateway such as LiteLLM switches to another provider.

24
00:01:33.967 --> 00:01:38.133
Responses keep the same shape, so failover is invisible to clients.

25
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:40.115
Let's recap.

26
00:01:40.115 --> 00:01:42.133
Use the cheapest capable layer.

27
00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:45.733
Cache, small model, frontier, and a self-hosted lane.

28
00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:48.900
Route with a cheap classifier, and audit misroutes.

29
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.442
Guard the cache against false hits.

30
00:01:51.442 --> 00:01:54.133
Next up: the fine-tune to deploy pipeline.
